COMMAND "Plant a @canonical Marker" DESCRIPTION "Plant the two-line `@canonical capability:<slug>` marker above any PUBLIC entry point so a cold agent's `grep '@canonical capability:'` LANDS on the one true implementation of a capability. One self-consuming REPLACE: a scaffy provenance line + the `@canonical` pointer line, injected directly above the head line the marker certifies. Works on Go funcs and Elixir public defs alike — the head line is taken VERBATIM as an opaque anchor, so multi-clause Elixir heads and brace-terminated Go signatures both anchor cleanly." LAST_UPDATED "17-07-2026-00-00-00" DOMAIN "barkpark" TAGS "docs", "cli", "search" CONCEPT "canonical-marker" VARIANT "plant-above-head" DIRECTION "add" VARIABLES
  VARIABLE 1 "TargetFile" OPAQUE TITLE "File holding the entry point to mark" DESCRIPTION "Repo-relative path of the source file whose public entry point becomes the canonical owner of a capability. OPAQUE: a path, never re-cased, exempt from path-casing lints — this file spells it {{.TargetFile}} throughout. The command is language-agnostic: any file whose comment syntax matches CommentToken (Go `//`, Elixir `#`)." EXAMPLES "internal/apiclient/client.go", "api/lib/barkpark/content/graph.ex"
  VARIABLE 2 "HeadLine" OPAQUE TITLE "The exact head line to mark (verbatim)" DESCRIPTION "The FULL source line of the public entry point, copied byte-for-byte from the file — the whole `func New(cfg Config) *Client {` or `def clamp_depth(nil), do: @default_depth`, never a name-prefix. This is the load-bearing lesson (D58): fenced anchors are LINE-granular, so a name-prefix anchor like `func New(` would clean-MISS the byte-exact match (D20) — the anchor must BE the whole line. OPAQUE: the line is re-emitted verbatim inside the payload, never re-cased. For a multi-clause Elixir function anchor the FIRST clause head — no two clause heads are byte-identical, so the full-line anchor is still unique (D20 exactly-once). WARNING 1: the line must occur EXACTLY ONCE in TargetFile (D20) — pick a longer, more specific head line if a bare signature repeats (a name-prefix like `func New(` matches ZERO times, since the fenced anchor is the whole line incl. its trailing newline, and is refused). WARNING 2 (indentation): copy the head WITH its leading whitespace — a `def` nested in a `defmodule` is 2-space indented, and only a byte-exact copy keeps that indent on the surviving head line. The two planted comment lines are emitted flush-left; `mix format` realigns leading comments to the following code's column on its next pass, so the planted marker settles to the head's indent (Go top-level funcs are column-0, so no realign is needed there)." EXAMPLES "func New(cfg Config) *Client {", "  def clamp_depth(nil), do: @default_depth", "  def render(assigns) do"
  VARIABLE 3 "slug" OPAQUE TITLE "The capability slug (kebab, unique repo-wide)" DESCRIPTION "The kebab-case capability name — the repo-wide-unique key in `@canonical capability:<slug>`. `grep -rn '@canonical capability:'` IS the index, so the slug must not already exist on any other impl (§8 invariant (a): a duplicate slug turns CI red). OPAQUE: one spelling per file, `{{.slug}}` everywhere — it names the capability, it is not a code identifier to be re-cased." EXAMPLES "api-client-new", "graph-depth-clamp", "preview-manifest"
  VARIABLE 4 "Aka" OPAQUE TITLE "aka: grep vocabulary (comma-list, no spaces)" DESCRIPTION "The `aka:` search words an agent actually types to find this code — comma-separated, NO spaces (so `depth,clamp,bound` lands a `grep clamp` on the marked head). OPAQUE literal: emitted verbatim into the marker line, never re-cased. Keep it to the words that would otherwise MISS the canonical impl (jargon names, error strings, alternate spellings)." EXAMPLES "New,client,constructor", "depth,clamp,bound,pagination", "og,opengraph,unfurl,share-card"
  VARIABLE 5 "CommentToken" OPAQUE TITLE "Line-comment token for the target language" DESCRIPTION "The host language's line-comment marker — `//` for Go/TS/JS, `#` for Elixir/Ruby/shell. OPAQUE literal: it prefixes both planted lines and is emitted verbatim. The command carries no language table; the caller states the token, keeping this one command usable across every surface." EXAMPLES "//", "#"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What this plants, and why it is a SELF-CONSUMING REPLACE (D58)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A canonical marker is two comment lines that must sit DIRECTLY ABOVE a public
# entry point, because scripts/docs-anchors-check.sh §8(b) asserts a public
# `def `/`func `/`export ` within 6 lines BELOW every `@canonical capability:`
# line. There is no stable structural anchor "one line above the head" to INSERT
# against — the only byte-exact, line-granular anchor is the head line ITSELF.
# So the op is a REPLACE of the head line WITH { provenance line + @canonical
# line + the same head line }. The head line survives as a substring of its own
# payload → this is a SELF-CONSUMING REPLACE, and E-005 requires it carry BOTH:
#
#   • a payload-derived ASLONG (idempotency rests on text the op writes), and
#   • a REANCHOR (run-≥2 keys on the planted mark family, never the mutated head).
#
# The guard is `capability:{{.slug}}` — the exact substring the @canonical line
# writes. On a re-run with the same slug the guard is already present → the whole
# op is skipped (the marker is planted once, never doubled). MARK VIRTUAL was
# REJECTED here (D58): the mark line IS plantable as a real comment, so a nominal
# handle would be strictly weaker than the receipt-backed real mark.
#
# INSERT BEFORE (prepend above a surviving head) is the primitive this idiom
# would prefer, but it is DEFERRED (D55) — this command ships TODAY on the proven
# self-consuming-REPLACE + ASLONG + REANCHOR idiom, which is fully expressible in
# the v2 grammar.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRE-RUN CHECK — the different-slug double-mark residual (D58)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The ASLONG guard is per-SLUG (`capability:{{.slug}}`). It refuses a re-plant of
# the SAME slug, but it is BLIND to a DIFFERENT-slug marker already sitting on the
# same head — a second run with a fresh slug would plant a second marker above the
# same entry point. Before running, grep the target for any existing marker:
#
#     grep -n '@canonical capability:' <TargetFile>
#
# If the head already carries a marker, DON'T run this — a public entry point owns
# at most one capability slug. This is the demand-driven doctrine (CLAUDE.md
# "Canonical-impl markers"): tag ONLY a genuinely-forked or jargon-named capability
# that a cold agent would otherwise have to find among decoys — a well-named,
# unforked function earns no marker, and a marker is REMOVED once dedup kills its
# decoys. The marker certifies "one owner," never "bug-free."
#
# Token spellings (D3d / A1): every variable here is OPAQUE, so each renders in
# exactly ONE spelling — no casing transforms. `{{.slug}}` is the capability name,
# never a code identifier; `{{.HeadLine}}` / `{{.TargetFile}}` / `{{.Aka}}` /
# `{{.CommentToken}}` are all verbatim literals.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OP — replace the head line with { provenance + @canonical + head } (D58)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The fenced anchor is the head line, byte-exact and line-granular (D20): it must
# occur EXACTLY ONCE in TargetFile. The payload re-emits it VERBATIM as its last
# line, with the two marker lines above. The provenance line plants the real mark
# `MARK:canonical-{{.slug}}` (D24 — a plain MARK requires its `MARK:<name>` text in
# the op's own payload; present here). ASLONG is the @canonical line's own
# `capability:{{.slug}}` substring (A4 — self-consuming, so idempotency rests
# ENTIRELY on this payload-derived guard; ASCII, brace-free, no `}}` beside a
# literal `}`). REANCHOR `canonical-` keys run ≥2 on the planted mark family
# (E-005 demands ASLONG + REANCHOR together on a self-consuming REPLACE).
IN "{{.TargetFile}}"
### Plant @canonical capability:{{.slug}} above the marked head
REPLACE
::: target head line :::
{{.HeadLine}}
::: target head line :::
WITH
::: {{.slug}} canonical marker :::
{{.CommentToken}} scaffy:add-canonical-marker {{.slug}} MARK:canonical-{{.slug}}
{{.CommentToken}} @canonical capability:{{.slug}} aka:{{.Aka}}
{{.HeadLine}}
::: {{.slug}} canonical marker :::
MARK "canonical-{{.slug}}"
REANCHOR "canonical-"
ASLONG FILE DONT CONTAIN "capability:{{.slug}}"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# POSTCONDITIONS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ASSERT FILE: both planted lines are present. Both strings are verbatim
# substrings the op writes and are guard-consistent with the ASLONG (A4/A5:
# ASCII, no `}}` adjacent to a literal `}`).
ASSERT FILE "{{.TargetFile}}" CONTAINS "@canonical capability:{{.slug}} aka:{{.Aka}}"
ASSERT FILE "{{.TargetFile}}" CONTAINS "MARK:canonical-{{.slug}}"

# ASSERT CMD (LOCAL): the §8(b) invariant in isolation — the marker sits on a
# PUBLIC entry point, i.e. a `def `/`func `/`export ` within 6 lines below the
# `@canonical capability:` line. `grep -A6` scopes to exactly the §8 window; this
# is the cheap, litter-immune local proof that the marker is well-placed.
ASSERT CMD "grep -A6 'capability:{{.slug}}' {{.TargetFile}} | grep -qE '^[[:space:]]*(def |func |export )'"

# ASSERT CMD (TIER ci): the FULL guard, §8(a) uniqueness + §8(b) placement across
# the whole tree. Deferred to CI — in the litter-saturated primary checkout
# docs-anchors-check.sh hangs past 115s scanning the tree (README clean-checkout
# caveat); CI runs it green in a fresh checkout in ~24s.
ASSERT CMD "bash scripts/docs-anchors-check.sh" TIER ci
